IMAGEVIEWER(1)                   User Commands                  IMAGEVIEWER(1)

NAME
       imageviewer - view, browse and edit pictures

SYNOPSIS
       imageviewer   [--fullscreen]   [--slideshow]   [--edit]  [--sort=order]
       [file|folder...]
       imageviewer --version

DESCRIPTION
       imageviewer is the desktop's picture viewer and editor, the program be-
       hind LikeOS Image Viewer in the Programs menu and  the  one  a  double-
       clicked  picture  in LikeOS Explorer opens in.  It shows PNG, JPEG, GIF
       (animated ones play), WebP, BMP, ICO, TIFF, the PNM family,  TGA,  XPM,
       XBM  and  ICNS  pictures,  turned the way the camera's EXIF orientation
       says, over a dark  backdrop  with  a  checkerboard  behind  transparent
       parts.

       One  file  opens  with  the  rest of its folder to browse, in the order
       LikeOS Explorer lists it: by name, numbers by their value ("IMG 2"  be-
       fore  "IMG 10").  Several files make a list of just those, in the order
       they were given.  A folder opens its  first  picture.   The  folder  is
       watched,  so  pictures  another program adds, changes or removes appear
       and disappear by themselves.

   The window
       The title row shows the picture's name and its place in the folder  ("3
       of  27")  and the commands: Edit, Rotate (to the right; with Shift held
       to the left), Delete (to the Trash), Open with (the other programs that
       take the picture's type, "Choose another app..." and  "Show  in  LikeOS
       Explorer"),  and the "more" menu: Save as, Copy, Copy path, Resize pic-
       ture, Set as desktop wallpaper, Send by mail,  Rename,  Settings,  Key-
       board shortcuts and About.

       A  dark  strip floats over the bottom of the picture: the filmstrip and
       slideshow switches, the picture's format, size and  file  size  at  the
       left, the previous and next picture in the middle, and at the right the
       info  panel,  fit or actual size, the zoom (as a menu of steps and as a
       slider) and full screen.  The filmstrip is a row of thumbnails  of  the
       folder above the strip; a click on one shows it.  Thumbnails are shared
       with  the other programs through the thumbnail cache in ~/.cache/thumb-
       nails.

       The info panel shows the file's name (type a new one and press Enter to
       rename the file), when the picture was taken and when the file was last
       changed (the pencil sets the file's date from a calendar; "Use as  file
       date" copies the time the camera recorded), the picture's size, resolu-
       tion,  depth,  colours  and  format, its folder (a click opens it), and
       what the camera recorded: camera and lens, exposure,  aperture,  sensi-
       tivity,  focal  length,  flash, orientation, software, position (in de-
       grees and in degrees, minutes and seconds) and colour space.

   Viewing
       The picture fits the window to begin with.  The mouse wheel zooms about
       the pointer (or, with the setting changed,  goes  through  the  folder;
       Ctrl  and  the wheel always zoom), dragging moves a picture larger than
       the window, a double click switches between fit and  actual  size.   In
       full  screen  the title row goes and the strips hide themselves after a
       moment without movement.  The slideshow goes through the folder in full
       screen, fading from one picture into the next.

       Rotating a JPEG file changes only its  orientation  tag,  so  it  loses
       nothing,  however  often  it  is  turned;  other  files are saved again
       turned.  A picture moved to the Trash can be brought back with the Undo
       of the message that says so.

   Editing
       Edit opens the editor in the same window: the tools in a  rail  at  the
       left  (Crop, Adjust, Styles, Draw, Retouch and Background), the picture
       in the middle, the tool's options at the right, and a  bar  above  with
       the  zoom, Undo, Redo, Revert (back to the unedited picture, after ask-
       ing), Compare (held down, it shows the  original),  Done  and  Discard.
       Every  change  is  a step that can be undone.  Done saves over the file
       (where the format can be written); its menu also saves a copy or copies
       the result to the clipboard.  Leaving with edits  that  are  not  saved
       asks whether to save them.  The original is never touched before one of
       these.

       Draw  marks  the picture up with a pen, a marker, a calligraphy pen and
       filled rectangles, and has an eraser that takes away whole strokes.   A
       rectangle  is  dragged  out  from corner to corner (a square with Shift
       held), for instance to cover a name or a  number  that  should  not  be
       seen;  a click chooses one, dragging moves it, its eight handles resize
       it, a colour recolours it and Delete removes it.  Saved  as  JPEG  with
       the camera data kept, the copy carries no thumbnail of the old pixels.

       Edits  are  worked  out on a smaller copy of the picture while they are
       made, and applied to the full picture when it is saved.  A picture with
       transparent parts saved as JPEG asks first whether to save a  PNG  copy
       instead or to fill them with white.

   Saving
       Save as writes a copy as PNG, JPEG (with its quality), WebP (lossy with
       its  quality,  or  lossless), TIFF or BMP.  JPEG copies keep the origi-
       nal's camera data unless that is switched off.  Resize picture saves  a
       smaller  copy,  "name  (resized)",  by the long side (640, 1280 or 1920
       pixels), to a size of one's own or by a percentage, with an estimate of
       the file's size, or replaces the original after asking.  Every file  is
       written  to  a temporary file first and then put in place, so a failure
       never leaves half a picture.

OPTIONS
       -f, --fullscreen
              Start in full screen.

       -s, --slideshow
              Start a slideshow of the folder.

       -e, --edit
              Open the picture in the editor.

       --sort=order
              The order of the folder: name (the  default),  name-desc,  date,
              date-desc, size, size-desc or type.

       --version
              Print the version and exit.

KEYS
       Left, Right, Page Up, Page Down
              The previous and the next picture.

       Home, End
              The first and the last picture.

       +, -, 0, 1
              Zoom in, zoom out, fit to the window, actual size.

       F11, F Full screen.

       F5, S  Slideshow;  Space  pauses  it  (outside a slideshow it shows the
              next picture), Esc ends it.

       T, I   The filmstrip, the info panel.

       E, R, Shift+R
              Edit, rotate right, rotate left.

       Delete, F2
              Move to the Trash, rename.

       Ctrl+S, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Shift+C, Ctrl+O
              Save as, copy the picture, copy its path, open another picture.

       Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, \
              In the editor: undo, redo, and (held) compare with the original.
              C, A, S, D, R and B choose the tools.

       F1     The list of keys.

FILES
       ~/.config/likeos/imageviewer.ini
              The settings: the backdrop, what the wheel does, the slideshow's
              pace, whether moving to the Trash asks first, the JPEG  quality,
              whether  camera data is kept, whether the filmstrip and the info
              panel show at start, whether going past the last  picture  comes
              back  to the first, and the window's size; and what the editor's
              tools keep from one run to the next: the  colour  and  width  of
              each  of  the  Draw tool's pens, the rectangles' colour, and the
              sizes of the Retouch and Background brushes.

       ~/.cache/thumbnails/normal
              The shared thumbnail cache the filmstrip reads and fills.

       /usr/share/applications/likeos-imageviewer.desktop
              The desktop entry, with the picture types the viewer opens.

SEE ALSO
       likeos-explorer(1), mediaplayer(1)

